1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,038
You'd never expect a face on Mars.

2
00:00:02,058 --> 00:00:06,191
But in 1976, NASA's Viking 1 photographed one,

3
00:00:06,211 --> 00:00:09,069
1.5 kilometers wide. What was it, actually?

4
00:00:09,089 --> 00:00:11,064
That's the first of six strange

5
00:00:11,084 --> 00:00:12,971
things found in our solar system.

6
00:00:12,991 --> 00:00:14,916
For each one, we'll look at what spacecraft

7
00:00:14,936 --> 00:00:17,385
and telescopes actually found, and give an honest

8
00:00:17,405 --> 00:00:20,444
verdict: solved, best guess, or still unknown.

9
00:00:20,464 --> 00:00:22,271
Number one: the face on Mars.

10
00:00:22,291 --> 00:00:26,186
In July 1976, NASA's Viking 1 orbiter was

11
00:00:26,206 --> 00:00:28,631
taking pictures of Mars, looking for a safe

12
00:00:28,651 --> 00:00:31,158
place to land its twin, Viking 2.

13
00:00:31,178 --> 00:00:34,229
In a region called Cydonia, one picture showed something

14
00:00:34,249 --> 00:00:36,561
odd. A huge rock formation that looked like a

15
00:00:36,581 --> 00:00:39,856
human head, with eyes, a nose, and a mouth.

16
00:00:39,876 --> 00:00:42,282
NASA's own caption at the time already explained

17
00:00:42,302 --> 00:00:44,659
it: the face was formed by shadows, giving

18
00:00:44,679 --> 00:00:47,499
the illusion of eyes, nose, and mouth.

19
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:49,953
The sun was low, at about 20 degrees. And

20
00:00:49,973 --> 00:00:52,847
the photo was full of tiny errors, which made

21
00:00:52,867 --> 00:00:54,900
it look even more like a face.

22
00:00:54,920 --> 00:00:56,475
But the picture took on a life of its

23
00:00:56,495 --> 00:01:00,360
own. Books, magazines, and later websites claimed it was

24
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:03,550
a monument built by an ancient Martian civilization.

25
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:06,058
More than twenty years later, new spacecraft

26
00:01:06,078 --> 00:01:08,353
went back with much sharper cameras.

27
00:01:08,373 --> 00:01:12,330
In 2001, NASA's Mars Global Surveyor photographed the

28
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:15,757
same spot. And in 2007, the Mars Reconnaissance

29
00:01:15,777 --> 00:01:18,079
Orbiter took an even closer look.

30
00:01:18,099 --> 00:01:19,560
The face was gone. In its place

31
00:01:19,580 --> 00:01:22,189
was an ordinary eroded hill, a mesa,

32
00:01:22,209 --> 00:01:24,259
like many others in the same region.

33
00:01:24,279 --> 00:01:27,567
NASA's conclusion: a natural looking Martian hill whose

34
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:31,257
illusory face-like appearance depends on the viewing angle

35
00:01:31,277 --> 00:01:32,477
and the angle of the light.

36
00:01:32,497 --> 00:01:35,793
Our brains are built to find faces everywhere.

37
00:01:35,813 --> 00:01:38,451
Scientists call it pareidolia. It's the same reason

38
00:01:38,471 --> 00:01:40,833
you sometimes see a face in a cloud

39
00:01:40,853 --> 00:01:42,016
or on the front of a car.

40
00:01:42,036 --> 00:01:43,876
Verdict: solved. It's a hill, and

41
00:01:43,896 --> 00:01:44,896
a trick of the light.

42
00:01:44,916 --> 00:01:47,360
Number two: the moon with two faces.

43
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:50,295
Saturn has a moon called Iapetus. And it looks

44
00:01:50,315 --> 00:01:52,503
like it was painted by two different artists.

45
00:01:52,523 --> 00:01:54,248
One side is as dark as coal. The

46
00:01:54,268 --> 00:01:57,183
other side is bright white, like fresh snow.

47
00:01:57,203 --> 00:02:01,066
Astronomers noticed this more than 300 years ago.

48
00:02:01,086 --> 00:02:05,587
Giovanni Cassini, who discovered Iapetus in 1671, could

49
00:02:05,607 --> 00:02:07,732
only see it on one side of Saturn.

50
00:02:07,752 --> 00:02:08,882
When the moon was on the other

51
00:02:08,902 --> 00:02:10,452
side of its orbit, it seemed to

52
00:02:10,472 --> 00:02:13,468
disappear. Its dark half was facing us.

53
00:02:13,488 --> 00:02:17,196
In 2007, NASA's Cassini spacecraft, named after

54
00:02:17,216 --> 00:02:19,637
him, flew close to Iapetus. And it

55
00:02:19,657 --> 00:02:22,081
found a strange runaway process.

56
00:02:22,101 --> 00:02:25,038
It starts with dust. Dark material spirals in from

57
00:02:25,058 --> 00:02:28,420
moons farther out from Saturn, and Iapetus runs into

58
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,172
it head-on, like a car driving into rain. That

59
00:02:31,192 --> 00:02:33,567
darkens the side that faces forward.

60
00:02:33,587 --> 00:02:35,712
Then the Sun takes over. A dark surface

61
00:02:35,732 --> 00:02:38,438
absorbs more sunlight and warms up. On the

62
00:02:38,458 --> 00:02:41,641
warm side, ice slowly turns into vapor.

63
00:02:41,661 --> 00:02:45,118
That vapor drifts away and freezes again on colder,

64
00:02:45,138 --> 00:02:48,091
brighter areas. So the bright parts get brighter, and

65
00:02:48,111 --> 00:02:50,911
the dark parts lose their ice and get darker.

66
00:02:50,931 --> 00:02:54,151
A Cassini scientist called Iapetus the victim of a

67
00:02:54,171 --> 00:02:57,479
runaway feedback loop, operating on a global scale.

68
00:02:57,499 --> 00:03:00,893
Iapetus has one more oddity: a giant ridge

69
00:03:00,913 --> 00:03:03,326
running along its equator, like the seam on

70
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:05,577
a walnut. That one is still being debated.

71
00:03:05,597 --> 00:03:08,292
But the two colors have a clear explanation.

72
00:03:08,312 --> 00:03:10,690
Verdict: solved. Dust from outside, and a

73
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:12,847
runaway game of ice and sunlight.

74
00:03:12,867 --> 00:03:14,950
Number three: the hexagon on Saturn.

75
00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:16,933
At Saturn's north pole, the clouds form

76
00:03:16,953 --> 00:03:19,586
a giant six-sided shape. A hexagon.

77
00:03:19,606 --> 00:03:21,874
It was first seen when the Voyager spacecraft

78
00:03:21,894 --> 00:03:25,344
flew past Saturn in 1980 and 1981.

79
00:03:25,364 --> 00:03:28,352
When Cassini arrived in 2004, the north

80
00:03:28,372 --> 00:03:30,904
pole was in its long winter darkness.

81
00:03:30,924 --> 00:03:33,536
Saturn's year lasts about 29 Earth years,

82
00:03:33,556 --> 00:03:36,068
so each season lasts more than seven.

83
00:03:36,088 --> 00:03:39,625
Cassini first saw the hexagon using infrared, which picks

84
00:03:39,645 --> 00:03:42,904
up heat from Saturn's warm interior. Later, when spring

85
00:03:42,924 --> 00:03:45,280
came, it finally saw it in sunlight.

86
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:48,900
The hexagon is about 30,000 kilometers across. That's more

87
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,819
than twice as wide as Earth. And winds inside

88
00:03:51,839 --> 00:03:54,779
it race at about 500 kilometers an hour.

89
00:03:54,799 --> 00:03:57,149
In the middle sits a giant storm,

90
00:03:57,169 --> 00:03:59,137
with an eye about fifty times larger

91
00:03:59,157 --> 00:04:00,895
than a typical hurricane on Earth.

92
00:04:00,915 --> 00:04:02,897
So what is the hexagon? It's a jet stream.

93
00:04:02,917 --> 00:04:06,723
A river of fast-moving air, bent into six sides.

94
00:04:06,743 --> 00:04:09,389
Scientists even made a hexagon in a lab,

95
00:04:09,409 --> 00:04:11,583
using a spinning tank of water. They also

96
00:04:11,603 --> 00:04:14,903
got squares, octagons, and other shapes, depending on

97
00:04:14,923 --> 00:04:16,430
the speed and the fluid.

98
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:18,599
But two questions remain. Why does Saturn have a

99
00:04:18,619 --> 00:04:20,982
hexagon only in the north, and not in the

100
00:04:21,002 --> 00:04:23,047
south? And why does it last so long?

101
00:04:23,067 --> 00:04:25,742
A Cassini scientist pointed out that weather features

102
00:04:25,762 --> 00:04:29,301
like this are notoriously turbulent and unstable. Yet

103
00:04:29,321 --> 00:04:32,116
the hexagon has held its shape for decades.

104
00:04:32,136 --> 00:04:34,393
Verdict: best guess. It's a jet stream,

105
00:04:34,413 --> 00:04:36,537
but nobody knows why it's so stable.

106
00:04:36,557 --> 00:04:38,964
Number four: the visitor from another star.

107
00:04:38,984 --> 00:04:41,372
On the nineteenth of October 2017, a

108
00:04:41,392 --> 00:04:44,267
telescope in Hawaii spotted a small object

109
00:04:44,287 --> 00:04:46,175
racing away from the Sun.

110
00:04:46,195 --> 00:04:48,075
It was moving too fast to be from

111
00:04:48,095 --> 00:04:50,590
our solar system. It was the first object

112
00:04:50,610 --> 00:04:53,559
ever confirmed to come from interstellar space.

113
00:04:53,579 --> 00:04:56,669
It was named ʻOumuamua, a Hawaiian word for scout.

114
00:04:56,689 --> 00:04:58,826
It was small, somewhere between about 100

115
00:04:58,846 --> 00:05:01,322
and 1,000 meters long, and probably very

116
00:05:01,342 --> 00:05:03,361
elongated. And it was already leaving.

117
00:05:03,381 --> 00:05:06,105
Then astronomers noticed something strange. As it moved

118
00:05:06,125 --> 00:05:08,583
away from the Sun, it sped up slightly,

119
00:05:08,603 --> 00:05:11,099
more than gravity alone could explain.

120
00:05:11,119 --> 00:05:13,527
Comets do this. When the Sun warms them,

121
00:05:13,547 --> 00:05:16,147
they release gas, and the gas pushes them

122
00:05:16,167 --> 00:05:18,550
like a little rocket. But comets also grow

123
00:05:18,570 --> 00:05:20,989
a fuzzy glowing cloud and a tail.

124
00:05:21,009 --> 00:05:22,796
ʻOumuamua showed neither.

125
00:05:22,816 --> 00:05:25,434
That gap left room for wild ideas. One

126
00:05:25,454 --> 00:05:28,080
Harvard astronomer even explored the idea that it

127
00:05:28,100 --> 00:05:30,882
was artificial, a thin solar sail.

128
00:05:30,902 --> 00:05:33,378
In 2023, two scientists offered a

129
00:05:33,398 --> 00:05:35,492
simpler answer in the journal Nature.

130
00:05:35,512 --> 00:05:38,325
They suggested ʻOumuamua began as a lump

131
00:05:38,345 --> 00:05:40,645
of ice. During millions of years in

132
00:05:40,665 --> 00:05:43,547
interstellar space, cosmic rays turned some of

133
00:05:43,567 --> 00:05:46,704
that water ice into trapped hydrogen gas.

134
00:05:46,724 --> 00:05:48,892
When it came close to the Sun and warmed

135
00:05:48,912 --> 00:05:52,284
up, that hydrogen escaped. Too little to see, but

136
00:05:52,304 --> 00:05:55,306
enough to give a small object a gentle push.

137
00:05:55,326 --> 00:05:59,066
Lab experiments show this kind of hydrogen does form

138
00:05:59,086 --> 00:06:01,385
when ice is hit by radiation. But we can't

139
00:06:01,405 --> 00:06:04,324
go back and check. ʻOumuamua is long gone.

140
00:06:04,344 --> 00:06:07,516
Verdict: best guess. Probably an icy object

141
00:06:07,536 --> 00:06:10,237
releasing hydrogen. It's the best explanation so

142
00:06:10,257 --> 00:06:12,577
far, but it hasn't been confirmed.

143
00:06:12,597 --> 00:06:14,666
Number five: the methane on Mars.

144
00:06:14,686 --> 00:06:16,894
On Earth, a lot of methane is made by

145
00:06:16,914 --> 00:06:20,389
living things, like microbes. So when NASA's Curiosity rover

146
00:06:20,409 --> 00:06:23,779
detected methane on Mars, people got excited.

147
00:06:23,799 --> 00:06:25,474
It could mean microbes. Or it could come

148
00:06:25,494 --> 00:06:28,422
from rocks, water, and heat deep underground.

149
00:06:28,442 --> 00:06:32,087
Curiosity measures tiny amounts in Gale Crater, less

150
00:06:32,107 --> 00:06:34,107
than half a part per billion on average.

151
00:06:34,127 --> 00:06:36,294
NASA compared it to a pinch of salt

152
00:06:36,314 --> 00:06:37,714
in an Olympic swimming pool.

153
00:06:37,734 --> 00:06:40,509
But sometimes, the methane suddenly jumps, up

154
00:06:40,529 --> 00:06:42,204
to about 20 parts per billion.

155
00:06:42,224 --> 00:06:44,787
Then the European Trace Gas Orbiter arrived. It was

156
00:06:44,807 --> 00:06:47,337
built to be the gold standard for measuring gases

157
00:06:47,357 --> 00:06:49,902
on Mars. And it saw no methane at all.

158
00:06:49,922 --> 00:06:52,639
The scientist leading Curiosity's methane instrument

159
00:06:52,659 --> 00:06:54,493
said: I was definitely shocked.

160
00:06:54,513 --> 00:06:56,195
So who was right? Maybe both.

161
00:06:56,215 --> 00:06:59,297
Curiosity's instrument needs a lot of power, so

162
00:06:59,317 --> 00:07:01,188
it mostly works at night. At night, the

163
00:07:01,208 --> 00:07:03,795
Martian air is calm, so methane seeping from

164
00:07:03,815 --> 00:07:06,040
the ground can build up near the surface.

165
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:07,873
During the day, the Sun stirs the

166
00:07:07,893 --> 00:07:10,054
air, and the methane gets mixed so

167
00:07:10,074 --> 00:07:12,250
thin that the orbiter can't see it.

168
00:07:12,270 --> 00:07:13,932
That explains part of the puzzle. It

169
00:07:13,952 --> 00:07:16,290
doesn't explain where the methane comes from,

170
00:07:16,310 --> 00:07:17,985
or why it spikes, or why it

171
00:07:18,005 --> 00:07:19,617
doesn't seem to build up over time.

172
00:07:19,637 --> 00:07:22,452
Verdict: still unknown. The timing makes sense,

173
00:07:22,472 --> 00:07:24,360
but the source is a mystery.

174
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:27,225
Number six: a gas in the clouds of Venus.

175
00:07:27,245 --> 00:07:29,383
Venus is a brutal place. The surface

176
00:07:29,403 --> 00:07:31,140
is hot enough to melt lead.

177
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:33,410
But high up in its clouds, the temperature

178
00:07:33,430 --> 00:07:36,624
and pressure are closer to Earth's. Some scientists

179
00:07:36,644 --> 00:07:39,268
have wondered if microbes could survive there.

180
00:07:39,288 --> 00:07:41,426
In September 2020, a team announced they

181
00:07:41,446 --> 00:07:44,458
had detected phosphine in Venus's clouds, using

182
00:07:44,478 --> 00:07:47,341
the ALMA and JCMT radio telescopes.

183
00:07:47,361 --> 00:07:49,757
On Earth, phosphine is linked to life.

184
00:07:49,777 --> 00:07:51,970
And no known non-living process on a

185
00:07:51,990 --> 00:07:54,459
rocky planet makes it in large amounts.

186
00:07:54,479 --> 00:07:55,866
The news went around the world.

187
00:07:55,886 --> 00:07:58,086
Then other teams checked the data. They found

188
00:07:58,106 --> 00:08:01,762
calibration problems. When ALMA released corrected data, the

189
00:08:01,782 --> 00:08:05,141
phosphine signal became much weaker, around one part

190
00:08:05,161 --> 00:08:07,661
per billion instead of the twenty first reported.

191
00:08:07,681 --> 00:08:09,474
Some researchers argued the signal

192
00:08:09,494 --> 00:08:11,631
could be sulfur dioxide instead.

193
00:08:11,651 --> 00:08:14,852
The original team still says no other molecule fits

194
00:08:14,872 --> 00:08:18,154
as well. Others have proposed volcanoes as a source.

195
00:08:18,174 --> 00:08:20,049
So, is there phosphine on Venus

196
00:08:20,069 --> 00:08:22,514
or not? Scientists are still arguing.

197
00:08:22,534 --> 00:08:25,819
Verdict: still unknown. The detection is disputed,

198
00:08:25,839 --> 00:08:27,739
and future missions will have to check.

199
00:08:27,759 --> 00:08:29,317
So, let's check the scoreboard.

200
00:08:29,337 --> 00:08:31,564
The face on Mars: solved. A hill

201
00:08:31,584 --> 00:08:32,659
and a trick of the light.

202
00:08:32,679 --> 00:08:35,798
Iapetus's two faces: solved. Dust and a

203
00:08:35,818 --> 00:08:38,151
runaway loop of ice and sunlight.

204
00:08:38,171 --> 00:08:40,191
Saturn's hexagon: best guess. A

205
00:08:40,211 --> 00:08:42,443
jet stream, but strangely stable.

206
00:08:42,463 --> 00:08:46,961
ʻOumuamua: best guess. Probably an icy body releasing hydrogen.

207
00:08:46,981 --> 00:08:49,409
Methane on Mars: still unknown.

208
00:08:49,429 --> 00:08:51,956
Phosphine on Venus: still unknown.

209
00:08:51,976 --> 00:08:54,233
Two solved, two best guesses, and two

210
00:08:54,253 --> 00:08:56,128
we still can't explain. Our own solar

211
00:08:56,148 --> 00:08:58,430
system still has plenty of secrets.

212
00:08:58,450 --> 00:09:00,862
NASA images are credited on screen. The 3D

213
00:09:00,882 --> 00:09:03,744
scenes are illustrations based on the sources.

214
00:09:03,764 --> 00:09:05,327
Which one surprised you the most?

215
00:09:05,347 --> 00:09:06,497
Tell me in the comments.
